---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/docs/Callout.astro";
import LinkCards from "../../components/docs/LinkCards.astro";
import { docHref } from "../../data/docs";
---

<DocsLayout slug="cloud">
  <p>
    OpenOrc Cloud runs your threads on machines that OpenOrc runs for you, so an agent keeps working while your Mac sleeps or is closed. It is a paid service with an account. Everything else in OpenOrc works without one, on your own computer.
  </p>

  <section aria-labelledby="what">
    <h2 id="what">What OpenOrc Cloud adds</h2>
    <p>
      A thread on your computer stops when the computer sleeps, because the agent runs there. A cloud thread runs its agent on a Linux machine of yours in OpenOrc Cloud instead. The conversation, the code, and the agent's work live
      there, so you can follow and answer the thread from OpenOrc on any of your computers, or from your phone, while the Mac that started it is asleep.
    </p>
    <p>OpenOrc Cloud also holds what people share in an organization:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>An organization's projects and their cloud threads, which every member can read, and where a teammate can write in the same thread. See <a href={docHref("together")}>Working together</a>.</li>
      <li>Team tasks, which can be assigned to a person and worked on in a cloud thread.</li>
      <li>Team chat: channels, group chats, and direct messages. See <a href={docHref("chat")}>Team chat</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Cloud threads support Claude Code, Codex, and OpenCode. Other agents run on your computer only.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="account">
    <h2 id="account">Sign in</h2>
    <p>Sign-up is by invitation for now. Someone who uses OpenOrc Cloud invites you to an organization by email, and you sign in with that address.</p>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>Open Settings → <strong>Account</strong> and click <strong>Sign in</strong>. OpenOrc opens its sign-in page in your browser, and the row says <strong>Continue in your browser</strong>.</li>
      <li>Enter your email address on the page, then the code that OpenOrc mails to you.</li>
      <li>The browser hands you back to the app. Settings → <strong>Account</strong> shows your name, your plan, and <strong>OpenOrc Cloud</strong> as <strong>Connected</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      If you close the page before you finish, click <strong>Open again</strong>, or <strong>Cancel</strong> to stop. <strong>Edit</strong> changes your name and picture, which your teammates see. <strong>Sign out</strong> signs this computer
      out. If your session ends on its own, the row says <strong>Your session ended</strong> and you sign in again.
    </p>
    <p class="docs-source">Source: <code>apps/desktop/src/renderer/src/views/settings-account.tsx</code>, <code>packages/hosted/src/account.ts</code></p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="pro">
    <h2 id="pro">Pro and cloud hours</h2>
    <p>
      An account without Pro can sign in and accept invitations, but cloud threads, and an organization's projects, threads, tasks, and chat, need Pro. Settings → <strong>Cloud</strong> appears once you have it. You get Pro in one of two
      ways:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>You pay.</strong> Under <strong>Plan</strong> in Settings → <strong>Account</strong>, choose to pay monthly or yearly and click <strong>Upgrade to Pro</strong>. Payment happens on a checkout page in your browser, and the
        row says <strong>Waiting for your payment</strong> until it arrives. <strong>Manage billing</strong> opens the same service's page, where you change the plan, update a card, or cancel.
      </li>
      <li><strong>An organization gives you a seat.</strong> The plan then says who pays, such as <strong>Paid by Birch Studio</strong>. See <a href={docHref("organizations") + "#seats"}>Seats</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Pro includes cloud hours each month. An hour counts for each hour a machine is awake for you, whichever of your threads it serves. A machine sleeps a short while after nothing needs it, and you use no hours while it sleeps. If the
      month's hours run out, buy more from the <strong>Cloud hours</strong> row. Hours you buy are used after the month's.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can see what is left in three places: <strong>Cloud hours</strong> in Settings → <strong>Account</strong>, the foot of the sidebar, which reads like <strong>Pro · 41 h left</strong>, and the <strong>OpenOrc Cloud</strong> row
      when you choose where a new thread works.
    </p>
    <Callout>
      When a cloud thread can't start a turn, a card above its composer says why and leads to the fix: <strong>Upgrade</strong>, <strong>Buy more hours</strong>, <strong>Update payment</strong>, or <strong>Sign in</strong> for an agent.
      A message sent into a turn that is already running never needs hours of its own.
    </Callout>
    <p class="docs-source">Source: <code>apps/desktop/src/renderer/src/views/AccountSeatPlan.tsx</code>, <code>packages/client/src/account-words.ts</code>, <code>packages/client/src/hosted-send.ts</code></p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="limits">
    <h2 id="limits">Prices, hours, and limits</h2>
    <p>These are the numbers OpenOrc Cloud uses today. They can change, and the app always shows the current prices before you pay.</p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">What</th><th scope="col">Now</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Pro, paid monthly</td><td>20 USD a month, per person</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Pro, paid yearly</td><td>200 USD a year, per person</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cloud hours included with Pro</td><td>100 each month. They reset each month, and unused hours don't carry over.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Extra cloud hours</td><td>50 hours for 10 USD. They last 12 months from the purchase.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>How long a machine stays awake once nothing needs it</td><td>2 minutes. Those minutes count toward your hours.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>How long a quiet terminal keeps a machine awake</td><td>30 minutes with nothing typed or printed. Then the terminal ends.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Your threads working on one machine at once</td><td>4. A fifth starts on another machine of yours.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A seat taken back, or a seat whose payment failed</td><td>The person keeps Pro for 1 more day.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>People in one invitation</td><td>Up to 30</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A thread's saved work and conversation</td><td>Kept for 30 days after the thread was last used</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A machine you stop using</td><td>Removed after 30 days. Its threads come back from their saved work.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A thread's folder left idle on its machine</td><td>Cleared after 7 days to free the disk. The next message makes it again from the saved work.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A file attached to a message and never sent</td><td>Deleted after 1 day</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="docs-source">Source: the OpenOrc Cloud server's billing constants and settings, and the products on its payment service</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="settings">
    <h2 id="settings">Set up the cloud on this computer</h2>
    <p>Open Settings → <strong>Cloud</strong>. Each section has one job:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>OpenOrc Cloud</strong> signs in each agent for your cloud turns, and connects your GitHub. See <a href={docHref("cloud-threads") + "#agents"}>Sign agents in for the cloud</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Check</strong> tries each thing a cloud turn needs and names the one that is broken.</li>
      <li><strong>Personal projects</strong>, and a list for the organization you are in, hold your cloud projects. See <a href={docHref("cloud-threads") + "#projects"}>Add a cloud project</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Skills</strong> chooses which of your skills go to your cloud turns.</li>
      <li><strong>Machines</strong> shows what is awake for you now, the hours it used, and <strong>Stop</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <a href={docHref("settings") + "#cloud"}>Settings</a> describes every row. Run <strong>Check</strong> first when something about cloud threads doesn't work. It tries <strong>Reach OpenOrc Cloud</strong>, <strong>Your Pro and hours</strong>,
      each agent's sign-in, <strong>Your GitHub</strong>, and <strong>Start a test machine</strong>, which costs you no hours.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="next-steps">
    <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
    <LinkCards cards={[{ slug: "cloud-threads" }, { slug: "organizations" }, { slug: "together" }]} columns={3} />
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
